You have already used `useState`. It is a **Hook**: a React function that connects a component to a capability such as state. A **custom Hook** is your own function that calls Hooks to reuse logic. Its name starts with `use` followed by a capital letter, such as `useCounter`.

These rules apply to the ordinary Hooks in this course, including `useState`, `useRef` and `useEffect`. React has other APIs with different rules; you do not need those APIs here.

## Keep the calls predictable

Make each **top-level Hook call** directly in a function component or custom Hook body, before any conditional return. Do not call them inside conditions, loops, event handlers, nested callbacks or ordinary utility functions. React must be rendering the component when these Hooks are called.

React associates a component's Hook state with the order of its calls. If a conditional Hook disappears on a later render, subsequent calls no longer line up. Giving the variables different names does not solve that problem.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: Labelled seats
> Imagine handing out numbered seats in the same order at each rehearsal.
>
> **How it maps:** A component's successive Hook calls need a consistent ordering, like the same people receiving the same numbered seats.
>
> **Where it stops:** React tracks calls within component instances; it does not inspect variable names or literally allocate theatre seats.

## Put the condition after the Hook

This complete component example assumes the React-enabled JSX environment from the first module. The import makes `useState` available; the environment transforms JSX before the browser executes it.

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter({
  hidden
}) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  if (hidden) return null;

  return (
    <button
      onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}
    >
      Count: {count}
    </button>
  );
}
```

When `hidden` changes from false to true, the component still calls its Hook and then returns nothing. When it becomes false again at the same tree position, its count is preserved. Moving the Hook below the conditional return would break the ordering rule.

An event handler may call `setCount`, because a setter is an ordinary function React returns to you. Calling `useState` inside that handler would be a new Hook call outside rendering. Distinguish creating the state during render from requesting an update later.

If each row needs local state, make a `Row` component and render rows with stable keys. Put its Hooks at the top of `Row`, rather than calling a Hook inside the parent's `map` callback. A React Hooks linter can catch many ordering and dependency mistakes before you run the app.

## Key takeaways

- Call ordinary Hooks at the top level of components or custom Hooks, before conditional returns.
- Hook order belongs to each component instance; variable names do not identify its state.
- Handlers may call setters; ordinary Hook calls belong in rendering.

## Further reading

- [React: Rules of Hooks](https://react.dev/reference/rules/rules-of-hooks)
- [React: State is tied to a position in the tree](https://react.dev/learn/preserving-and-resetting-state)

An **effect**, declared with `useEffect`, synchronises a component with something outside React: a browser event subscription, a connection or a non-React widget. Rendering calculates JSX; an effect performs external work after React commits a render. Effects run in the browser, not during server rendering.

Do not use an effect merely because state changed. Calculating a filtered list belongs in rendering. A purchase triggered by a particular click belongs in that event handler. An event subscription that must exist while a component is present is a good effect.

## Pair setup with cleanup

This complete component reports the last key pressed while it is mounted. `window.addEventListener` registers a browser callback. `removeEventListener` unregisters that same function.

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function LastKey() {
  const [key, setKey] = useState('None');

  useEffect(() => {
    function onKey(event) {
      setKey(event.key);
    }
    window.addEventListener(
      'keydown', onKey
    );
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener(
        'keydown', onKey
      );
    };
  }, []);

  return <p>Last key: {key}</p>;
}
```

The **setup** function registers the callback. The returned function is **cleanup**: React calls it when stopping this synchronisation. Return the function rather than calling it immediately. Removing a different function with similar code would not remove the original listener.

The empty dependency array means the effect reads no changing props or state. `setKey` has a stable identity, so it can be omitted here. The callback gets the key from the browser event instead of closing over `key`.

Trace the behaviour: mount the component, press A, and its state shows A. Remove the component, then press B. Cleanup has removed its listener, so that removed component no longer responds. A new mount creates its own subscription.

## Reconnecting and Strict Mode

For an effect with changing dependencies, React stops the previous setup before starting the new one: cleanup uses the old render's values, then setup uses the new values. Final removal also runs cleanup. Cleanup is therefore more than an unmount notification.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: A library loan
> Borrow a book when you need it, return it before replacing it, and return the last book when you leave.
>
> **How it maps:** Setup acquires an external resource; cleanup releases that particular resource before replacement or removal.
>
> **Where it stops:** Some effects require no cleanup, and React schedules these steps around commits rather than human visits.

When Strict Mode is enabled at the root during development, React runs an extra setup-and-cleanup cycle for effects before the ongoing setup. This helps expose missing cleanup. It is a development check, not a production promise about how many times a component renders. Correct code leaves only the intended active subscription and cleans it up when removed. Do not hide the check with a ref that prevents a later setup.

## Key takeaways

- Effects synchronise external systems; calculations and direct event responses usually belong elsewhere.
- Cleanup undoes the matching setup before replacement and on removal.
- Development Strict Mode helps find effects that do not clean up correctly.

## Further reading

- [React: Synchronising with Effects](https://react.dev/learn/synchronizing-with-effects)
- [React: useEffect](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect)
- [React: StrictMode](https://react.dev/reference/react/StrictMode)
- [MDN: removeEventListener](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/removeEventListener)

Network responses can arrive in a different order from requests. Request list A, then list B: if B finishes first and A finishes later, blindly storing both results makes A overwrite B. This **race condition** depends on timing, so a quick test on a fast connection may miss it.

This lesson uses a deliberately small **client-only** fetch example to teach effect lifecycle. Production applications may use a framework's data facilities or a data library for caching, request sharing and other needs. This example does not provide those facilities.

## Represent the request's outcome

The Fetch API's `fetch(url)` returns a **Promise**, an object representing a result that may arrive later. In an `async` function, `await` waits for that result without blocking the browser's whole JavaScript execution. `try...catch` handles failures: a thrown error in its try block transfers control to its catch block, including a rejected Promise encountered by `await`. The `Error` constructor creates the error object used with `throw` in this example. An HTTP error such as 404 does not itself reject fetch: check `response.ok`. `response.json()` asynchronously parses the response body as JSON.

Assume a same-origin teaching endpoint whose URL arrives as a string prop. It returns an array of objects with stable unique `id` and string `text` fields. The data contract is assumed; a real application should validate untrusted data. Complete component example, with JSX transformed by the environment:

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function Results({ url }) {
  const [view, setView] = useState({
    url: null, status: 'loading', items: []
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    let ignore = false;
    setView({
      url, status: 'loading', items: []
    });

    async function load() {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error('Request failed');
        }
        const items = await response.json();
        if (!ignore) {
          setView({
            url, status: 'success', items
          });
        }
      } catch {
        if (!ignore) {
          setView({
            url, status: 'error', items: []
          });
        }
      }
    }

    load();
    return () => { ignore = true; };
  }, [url]);

  if (view.url !== url ||
      view.status === 'loading') {
    return <p role="status">Loading…</p>;
  }
  if (view.status === 'error') {
    return (
      <p role="alert">Could not load.</p>
    );
  }
  if (view.items.length === 0) {
    return <p>No tasks found.</p>;
  }
  return (
    <ul>
      {view.items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

The inner async function handles the asynchronous work. The effect itself returns an ordinary cleanup function, not a Promise. A single status distinguishes loading, error and success; an empty successful array gets its own message. The stored URL prevents displaying a previous URL's data during a render before the new effect has started.

## Trace overlapping requests

Each setup gets its own local `ignore` variable. Changing from A to B runs A's cleanup, setting A's flag to true, and starts B with a separate false flag. If B succeeds first, its data is stored. If A later succeeds or fails, its flag prevents either branch from changing state. Unmounting similarly marks the active request obsolete.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: An old takeaway order
> You replace an order and disregard a later message about the cancelled choice.
>
> **How it maps:** Cleanup marks an earlier request obsolete so its late reply cannot replace the current result.
>
> **Where it stops:** The flag does not cancel network traffic or undo server work. It only prevents applying an obsolete result.

Ignoring and cancelling are different: `AbortController` can cancel a supported fetch, but this example only ignores obsolete outcomes. Test response order explicitly, including stale errors, loading, an empty array, a current error and removal before completion.

## Key takeaways

- Fetch needs an explicit HTTP success check and separate loading, error and empty handling.
- Cleanup marks obsolete work so late responses cannot replace the active result.
- This teaching pattern is not a complete production data layer.

## Further reading

- [React: Fetching data with Effects](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffect#fetching-data-with-effects)
- [MDN: Using Fetch](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch)
- [MDN: async function](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Statements/async_function)

A **custom Hook** is a function that uses React Hooks to provide a reusable behaviour. Name it for what it does, beginning with `use` followed by a capital letter. Give it clear inputs and return values. Calling it is part of rendering the caller; it must follow the same top-level ordering rules as built-in Hooks.

Do not turn every utility into a Hook. A plain function that only filters an array needs no special Hook name. Extract a Hook when it packages stateful or external synchronisation logic with a useful purpose.

## Share the recipe, not the state

Complete module example for a React-enabled JSX environment. A returned object's shorthand `{ count, increment }` means properties named `count` and `increment` containing those local values.

```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';

export function useCounter(step) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  function increment() {
    setCount(c => c + step);
  }
  return { count, increment };
}

function Counter({ step }) {
  const { count, increment } =
    useCounter(step);
  return (
    <button onClick={increment}>
      {count}
    </button>
  );
}

export default function Pair() {
  return (
    <>
      <Counter step={1} />
      <Counter step={2} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Click the first button twice: its value becomes 2 while the second stays 0. Click the second once: it becomes 2 without changing the first. Each call uses its own state, even though both execute the same Hook function. To share one count, move one Hook call to a common parent and pass its value and handlers down.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: One recipe, two kitchens
> Two cooks can follow the same recipe while keeping separate ingredients and meals.
>
> **How it maps:** A custom Hook shares the instructions for behaviour; each call has its own state and effects.
>
> **Where it stops:** A Hook may deliberately connect to a shared external source, so independent local state does not imply the outside world is separate.

## Keep synchronisation inside the Hook

A Hook that installs a browser listener must also remove it. The following complete Hook definition extracts the previous lesson's keyboard behaviour. A component calls `useLastKey()` at its top level and renders the returned string.

```jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function useLastKey() {
  const [key, setKey] = useState('None');
  useEffect(() => {
    function onKey(event) {
      setKey(event.key);
    }
    window.addEventListener(
      'keydown', onKey
    );
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener(
        'keydown', onKey
      );
    };
  }, []);
  return key;
}
```

Two components using this Hook both respond to the same keyboard because they observe the same browser, not because they share a state variable. Removing one component removes its subscription while the other continues working. Extraction should preserve the original behaviour, dependencies and cleanup.

The `step` input in `useCounter` is read from each current render. If a parent changes it, future clicks use the new step without resetting the count. Initial state is only initial state; passing different inputs to a Hook does not automatically mean resetting every value inside it.

## Key takeaways

- Custom Hooks share stateful logic; separate calls have independent state.
- Clear inputs and outputs make a Hook easier to read and test.
- Keep effect cleanup and accurate dependencies with the behaviour being extracted.

## Further reading

- [React: Reusing Logic with Custom Hooks](https://react.dev/learn/reusing-logic-with-custom-hooks)
- [React: Rules of Hooks](https://react.dev/reference/rules/rules-of-hooks)
- [MDN: Object initialiser shorthand](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Operators/Object_initializer)

Start with props: a child gets a value and callbacks from its parent. If many distant components need the same value, passing it through intermediate components can become repetitive. **Context** lets a component read a value supplied by an ancestor without each intermediate component forwarding a prop.

`createContext(defaultValue)` creates a context object outside components. A **provider** supplies its value to descendants. A **consumer** reads it with `useContext`. The context object identifies the channel; it does not hold a global state variable. Put changing data in state owned by an appropriate parent and provide that value.

## Read the nearest provider

Complete React 19 module example for a React-enabled JSX environment. React 19 supports `<ThemeContext value={...}>` as provider syntax. You may also see `<ThemeContext.Provider value={...}>` in existing code.

```jsx
import {
  createContext, useContext, useState
} from 'react';

const ThemeContext = createContext('light');

function ThemeLabel() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <p>Theme: {theme}</p>;
}

export default function ThemeDemo() {
  const [theme, setTheme] =
    useState('dark');
  return (
    <>
      <ThemeLabel />
      <ThemeContext value={theme}>
        <ThemeLabel />
        <ThemeContext value="light">
          <ThemeLabel />
        </ThemeContext>
      </ThemeContext>
      <button onClick={() => setTheme(
        t => t === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark'
      )}>
        Switch outer theme
      </button>
    </>
  );
}
```

Read the three labels in order. The first has no provider above it, so reads the default light. The second reads the outer provider's initial dark. The third reads the nearer inner provider's light. After switching the outer theme, the second becomes light; the first and third still read light for their original reasons.

React searches upwards from the component calling `useContext`. A provider returned by that same component is below the call, so it cannot affect that call. The default is a fallback when there is no matching provider, not a value that gets merged into every provider.

> [!analogy] Real-world analogy: A room's noticeboard
> A building has general notices, while an individual room can supply its own local notice.
>
> **How it maps:** A nearer provider overrides the value a consumer would otherwise get from a more distant ancestor.
>
> **Where it stops:** Context follows the React component tree, not physical distance or DOM styling rules.

## Updates still matter

When a provided value changes according to `Object.is`, React updates components reading that context. Wrapping a consumer in `memo` does not block fresh context. A new object used as a provider value has a new identity even if its fields look the same, so careless object creation can cause unnecessary updates.

Context transports a value; it does not decide state ownership or make mutation safe. Keep ordinary immutable state updates. Prefer a small relevant context over placing every unrelated app value in one changing object. For two nearby siblings, lifting state and passing props may be simpler and more explicit.

## Key takeaways

- Try props first; context is useful for values needed across distant descendants.
- Consumers read the nearest matching provider above them, or the default if none exists.
- Context updates reach consumers; context does not replace state ownership or immutability.

## Further reading

- [React: Passing Data Deeply with Context](https://react.dev/learn/passing-data-deeply-with-context)
- [React: useContext](https://react.dev/reference/react/useContext)
- [React: createContext](https://react.dev/reference/react/createContext)

A component can render because its own state changes, a parent renders it, or context it reads changes. A render recalculates the description of UI; it does not mean React rewrites every DOM element. First keep rendering pure, put state near the components that need it, and remove unnecessary effects.

If an interaction feels slow, reproduce it with realistic data and inspect it with React Developer Tools' **Profiler** or the browser's performance tools. Compare the same interaction before and after a change. Development checks can add work, so development timing is not a reliable substitute for production measurement. Small examples here teach behaviour, not a measured speedup.

## Three different things to cache

**Memoisation** reuses a previous result when its inputs are unchanged.

| Tool | What it may reuse |
|---|---|
| `memo` | A component's previous render work |
| `useMemo` | A calculated value |
| `useCallback` | A function identity |

`memo` wraps a component and normally compares each prop using `Object.is`. It can skip parent-driven re-rendering with unchanged props, but this is an optimisation, not a guarantee. Its own state and consumed context still update it. A freshly created array, object or callback is a different identity, even when its contents or code look the same.

## Cache a calculation only when worthwhile

Complete component example, assuming `tasks` is an array of objects with `done` booleans. This tiny calculation is illustrative; it has not been shown to need caching.

```jsx
import { useMemo } from 'react';

export default function Summary({ tasks }) {
  const remaining = useMemo(
    () => tasks.filter(t => !t.done),
    [tasks]
  );
  return (
    <p>{remaining.length} remaining</p>
  );
}
```

The calculation must be pure. Replacing a task means copying the changed object and array: if you mutate the existing array, its identity may stay the same and the cached result can become stale. `useMemo` does not make mutation safe. Removing `useMemo` and calculating the filter directly must preserve correctness.

`useCallback` returns a function without calling it. Component-body excerpt, assuming `useCallback` is imported and `setTasks` is the stable `useState` setter:

```jsx
const toggle = useCallback(id => {
  setTasks(items => items.map(item =>
    item.id === id
      ? { ...item, done: !item.done }
      : item
  ));
}, []);
```

The functional update receives current tasks, so this callback does not read a captured `tasks` value. If a callback reads changing props or state, those inputs belong in its dependencies. A stable callback can help when passed to a memoised child; adding one everywhere adds complexity without establishing a benefit.

## Keep caches optional

React may discard cached values or render a memoised component again. Do not put an essential connection or side effect inside `useMemo`, or rely on it to retain application state. An optimisation must preserve behaviour if React recalculates it.

React Compiler can automatically memoise components, values and functions when configured in a supporting build. It is a build tool, not something guaranteed merely by importing React. Check the project's build configuration before adding manual memoisation. Correctness, immutability and useful measurements still come first.

## Key takeaways

- Reproduce and measure slow interactions before adding caches.
- `memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` cache different things and do not guarantee correctness or render counts.
- Immutable updates and accurate dependencies matter with or without memoisation.

